Question to deepseek: python csv is it more efficient to wright the whole file instead of updating only the row that change

For standard CSV file operations in Python, writing the entire file is almost always the correct and more practical approach, even though it feels inefficient. Directly overwriting a single row is technically very difficult and only marginally beneficial in specific edge cases.

🔍 Why You Can't Simply "Update" One Row
A CSV file is a plain text file. To "update" a specific row, you would need to locate it and overwrite the exact same number of bytes on disk. This is problematic for two key reasons:

Variable Line Length: If your new value has a different number of characters (e.g., changing "Bob" to "Robert"), the byte length of that line changes. Overwriting it would corrupt the formatting of the rest of the file.

No Row Indexing: The file system has no concept of "row 500." The only way to find a row is to read every byte from the start until you reach it.

As a result, the standard pattern for updating a CSV is to read all the old data into memory, modify it, and then write all the new data back to the file.

